///18087
  Indicates  to  Concurrent that the next COM or EXE program  in  the  current
  window makes use of an 8087 coprocessor.

  Syntax:
  8087
  8087=ON|OFF

  Explanation:
  The  8087  command invokes the 8087 coprocessor used  exclusively  for  math
  applications.   Most  commands and applications do not make use of the 8087.
  The 8087 has two settings,  ON and OFF.  The default setting is OFF.  An  ON
  setting  lasts  for  the current window only until  you  enter  an  8087=OFF
  command or you turn off your computer.

  The  8087  command is only  necessary  for   DOS,  8087-dependent  programs.
  CP/M  programs indicate in their command file header if they require an 8087
  to run.   See the CHSET command description for more information on  running
  8087-dependent CP/M programs.

///1ACTIONKEYS
During buffered command-line input:
  Left / Ctrl-A       Move left
  Right / Ctrl-F      Move right
  Home / End          Beginning / end of line
  Up / Ctrl-W         Recall the last line on empty input; otherwise End
  Backspace / Rubout  Delete left
  Delete / Ctrl-G     Delete at the cursor
  Ctrl-K / Ctrl-X     Delete to end / beginning
  Ctrl-B              Toggle Home/End
  Ctrl-U              Cancel the line with the historical #/newline echo
  Ctrl-R              Retype the line
  Ctrl-E              Continue on another screen row
  Return              Accept the complete buffer

Ctrl-Alt-1..4 selects PC consoles; Ctrl-Alt-Tab cycles them.
After installing WMENU, Ctrl-Alt-Esc opens its menu.
Ctrl-keypad-Plus remains an alternative WMENU shortcut.
Ctrl-C cancels commands that enable the normal console-abort behavior.
Individual applications can use their own key bindings.

///1ADDMEM
  Use  the  ADDMEM  command to increase the amount of memory  allocated  to  a
  program with an EXE file extension.

  Syntax:
  ADDMEM
  ADDMEM=n

  Explanation:
  ADDMEM  allocates  additional memory for the current window only.   If you
  receive a "not enough memory" message when trying to load an .EXE file, use
  ADDMEM before loading the program again.  If you do not know how much
  memory to add, start with the default allocation (16K) and increase until the
  program loads.  (For EXE programs  that always require an
  extra allocation,  make a BATCH  file  that runs   ADDMEM  and  invokes  the
  application.)   To  change the  default allocation, enter  ADDMEM
  with  a  value of 0 to  999,  indicating  the  number   of  kilobytes
  of additional memory necessary to run  the
  program.   See the "Running Applications Under Concurrent PC DOS" supplement
  for information on addmem requirements for many popular applications.

  EXAMPLES:
  ADDMEM<Enter>
  ADDMEM=32<Enter>

///1ASM80
ASM80 is the translated CP/M-80 assembler used to build 8080 programs.
It is a development tool, separate from the native 8086 ASM86 assembler.
Its source/output conventions follow CP/M-80 ASM; HEXCOM converts Intel
HEX output to a flat COM file.

///1asm86
    Syntax:

    ASM86 filespec {$options}

    Purpose:

    ASM-86 assembles assembly language statements, producing  a  file
    in hexadecimal format, a print file and a symbol table file.  The
    assumed filetype of  source  and  included  files  is  A86.   The
    special  characters  X,  Y  and Z indicate output to the console,
    output to the printer,  and  zero  output,  respectively.  If  no
    format is specified, Digital Research format (FD) is assumed.

    ///2Examples
    Examples:

        A>ASM86 PROG
        A>ASM86 PROG $SZ PX
        A>ASM86 PROG $HB PY AC

    ///2Options
    Syntax:

    ASM86 filespec ($Ad Hd Pd Sd Fd)

    A   source file drive - .A86
    d = (logical drives A-D)

    H   hex file drive    - .H86
    d = (logical drives A-D, X,Y,Z)

    P   print file drive  - .LST
    d = (logical drives A-D, X,Y,Z)

    S   symbol file drive - .SYM
    d = (logical drives A-D, X,Y,Z)

    F   format of hex file- .H86
    d = (D=Digital Research, I=Intel)

///1BACK
  See the BACKREST command.

///1BACKREST
  BACKREST consists of two separate commands,  BACK and REST.   A description
  of BACK is presented first.

  BACK  copies  one  or  more files from a hard disk  to  a  floppy  disk  for
  archiving  purposes or to protect against hardware malfunction or accidental
  erasure.

  Syntax:
  BACK
  BACK FULL
  BACK CONTROL=n
  BACK REPORT

  Explanation:
  Use  the  BACK  options to specify how your hard disk files  are  copied  to
  backup disks.

  BACK  operates  according to parameters specified in  the  CONTROL.BR  file.
  The  parameters in CONTROL.BR can be used as is or can be modified  to  suit
  your  particular  needs--see  Appendix  C  in  the  User's  Guide  for  more
  information.   BACK   directs   you   to insert a formatted  disk  into  the
  destination  drive.   When a disk is full,   BACK displays its unique volume
  number so that you can label  it  appropriately.   BACK asks for more backup
  disks  as  it  requires them.    BACK backs up only new files or those which
  have been modified since the last backup operation.

  BACK  FULL  backs  up all hard disk files that  satisfy  the  parameters  in
  CONTROL.BR.  Backup diskette numbering continues in sequence.

  BACK  CONTROL=n takes its instructions from a specific control file  of  the
  form  CONTROLn.BR.    The letter  n  indicates any character that  satisfies
  Concurrent's  file-naming conventions.    All  files  and  backup disks used
  with  BACK  CONTROL=n  are identified with n and the backup date.

  BACK REPORT prints a report of backup operations.  Use this command to print
  a record of previous backup operations.

  The following is a description of REST.

  REST restores a set of archived files from a floppy disk to a hard disk.

  Syntax:
  REST
  REST CONTROL=n
  REST REPORT

  Explanation:
  REST   restores   hard  disk  files according to  the  parameters   in   the
  CONTROL.BR  file.   REST  displays the BackRest Restore Facility Menu  which
  restores  bad  files  automatically and also prompts you for  the  drive  to
  restore,  path name or user number that originally contained the files,  the
  names of the files to restore, and the date the files were backed up.

  REST  CONTROL=n  restores your hard disk files according to  the  parameters
  in a specific BackRest control file of the form CONTROLn.BR.    The letter n
  indicates    any   character   that   satisfies    Concurrent    file-naming
  conventions.    Specific  CONTROL.BR   files  are  modified  copies  of  the
  default   control   file.    A  specific  control  file  includes  an  extra
  character in its filename.

  REST REPORT prints a report of previous restore operations.

///1BATCH
filename.BAT {argument1} ... {argument9}
Executes a text file of commands through BATCH.CMD. %1 through %9 are
replaced with supplied arguments. BAT files can chain to another BAT file.

BATCH shares DIR and other TMP built-ins, including CLS, USER, PRINTER,
PAUSE and REM, and the CD/MD/RD, DEL/DELQ and RENAME aliases. DEL invokes
native ERA.CMD. Ordinary program lines use the executable search order.
This is not a full MS-DOS COMMAND.COM scripting language: IF, GOTO, FOR,
ECHO and SHIFT are not implemented by this BATCH.

Native command/substitution files for SUBMIT use the separate .SUB format.

///1BRINST
BRINST prepares CONTROL.BR for the BACK/REST backup utilities. Follow its
prompts for source paths, backup choices and exceptions. BACK looks for
CONTROL.BR on the current drive. See BACKREST for backup/restore commands.

///1CD
CD is a shell/BATCH alias for CHDIR.CMD. See CHDIR for syntax.

///1CHDIR
  CHDIR and its options provide access to subdirectories on DOS media.

  Syntax:
  CHDIR {d:}
  CHDIR {d:} {\} {dirname} {\dirname...}
  CHDIR fd:={d:} {\} {dirname} {\dirname...}

  Explanation:
  The  first  form  above displays the  sequence,  or  path,  of  hierarchical
  subdirectories that leads to the current subdirectory.   Use the second form
  to  make  the last subdirectory in the path the current  directory  for  the
  current  drive  or the drive specified.   The third form is used  to  assign
  floating  drive (fd:) name N or O to the subdirectory specified.   CHDIR can
  be abbreviated to CD in the command line.

///1CHSET
  Syntax:
  CHSET [HELP]
  CHSET {d:}filename{.CMD}
  CHSET {d:}filename{.CMD} [field=setting{,field=setting,...}]

  Explanation:
  CHSET is a command that allows you to make changes in the command header  of
  a program file with a  .CMD file extension.  CHSET has three fields that you
  can display or change.  The three fields and their possible settings are:

  8087  - The  8087  field has three  possible  settings:   ON,  OFF,  or  OPT
  (optional).    Specify  ON  for programs that require an 8087 processor   in
  order  to run.   Choose OPT when you have a program that  can use an 8087 if
  it  is  present in your system,  or emulate an  8087 if it is  not  present.
  Specify OFF if you want to cancel a previous ON or OPT selection.

  SHARED - The SHARED field has two settings,  ON or OFF.  Select ON when  you
  want  to  modify  a  program's command header so that the  program  can  use
  Concurrent's  shared code  capabilities.   Specify OFF if you want to cancel
  a previous ON selection.

  SUSPEND - The SUSPEND field has two options, ON or OFF.  When you select ON,
  CHSET  modifies a  program's command header so that Concurrent suspends that
  program's operation when it occupies a switched-out window.   OFF cancels  a
  previous ON selection.

  Changing Field Settings
  You  can  change any or all of CHSET's fields in a single command line.   To
  change   a  setting for a field or fields,  enter a CHSET command   with   a
  filespec and the fields you want to change.  For example, if you type

  B>chset calc.cmd [8087=opt,shared=on,suspend=on]

  CHSET  modifies  the command  header of the program contained  in  the  file
  CALC.CMD.

  Displaying Field Settings
  When you enter a CHSET command with a filespec and no field settings,  CHSET
  displays the settings for the specified file(s).  For example, if you type

  B>chset erase.cmd

  CHSET responds with the following display:

  B:ERASE   .CMD settings are [8087=OFF,SHARED=OFF,SUSPEND=OFF]

  Modifying a command header is not a trivial matter.  While CHSET cannot harm
  your .CMD files,  inappropriate use of it can cause your programs to  behave
  in unpredictable ways.

  CHSET [HELP]

  If you type HELP in  square  brackets following the CHSET command keyword in
  a  command  line,  CHSET  responds  with  a display  giving  you   a   brief
  explanation   of  CHSET  and  a list of examples of correct   command   line
  syntax.   You  can also type CHSET without a command tail to summon  CHSET's
  HELP display.

  ///2EXAMPLES
  Examples:

  A>chset taxaid.cmd [shared=on,8087=opt,suspend=off]

  The  above command modifies the command header of a program called TAXAID so
  that  it can use Concurrent's facilities for shared code and can either  use
  an  8087  if  it is present in the system or emulate an 8087 if  it  is  not
  present.  The command also turns off the  SUSPEND  field for TAXAID, so that
  TAXAID can run when it is in  a  switched-out  window.  The inclusion of the
  .CMD filetype is optional.

  A>chset *

  The  above command displays the current settings for every CMD command  file
  in  the current directory.   CHSET does not accept a filespec of *.* because
  it accepts only a blank filetype or filetype .CMD.

  B>chset ezesort [8087=on]

  The   above   command  modifies  the  command  header for a  program  called
  EZESORT so that it can use an installed 8087 processor.

  A>chset rectify.cmd

  The   above   command causes CHSET to respond with a display of the  current
  settings of the fields in RECTIFY.CMD's command header.

///1CLS
CLS
Clears the current console and homes its cursor. CLS is a TMP/PCTMP
shell built-in and is also recognized by CDOS BATCH. No CLS.CMD or
separate CLS RSP is required. An explicit extension selects disk lookup.

///1CNTRLCHARS
See ACTIONKEYS for the current command-line editing and console keys.
Applications and raw console input can assign different meanings.

///1COMMANDS
Concurrent DOS 4.1 command reference for this distribution.

Shell built-ins (PCTMP, also shared with BATCH):
  drive letters A:..P:, USER, DIR, CLS, ORDER, COMSIZE, ADDMEM,
  8087, SUSPEND, PRINTER, PAUSE, REM and SYS.
  RUNMENU activates the interactive shell's linked menu interface.
  STOP name window stops a process; bare STOP uses STOP.CMD.

Aliases:
  CD -> CHDIR.CMD    MD -> MKDIR.CMD    RD -> RMDIR.CMD
  DEL -> ERA.CMD     DELQ -> ERAQ.CMD   RENAME -> REN.EXE

Native commands supplied across the seven disks:
  ASM80 ASM86 BACK BATCH BRINST CHDIR CHSET COPYMENU DATE DCOMP DCOPY
  DIR DISKSTAT DREDIX DSKMAINT DSP ED EDITMENU ERA ERAQ FM FUNCTION
  GENCCPM GENCMD GENLDR GET HDMAINT HELP HEXCOM INITDIR MKDIR N PIP
  PRINT PRINTMGR PUT RDR REN REST RMDIR SDIR SET SETPORT SETUP SHOW
  SPL STOP SUBMIT SYSDISK SYSTAT WINDOW WMENU

DOS executables: COPY.EXE DATE.EXE ERASE.EXE PATH.EXE REN.EXE TIME.EXE.
LOADCCPM.COM loads Concurrent from DOS; REDOS.COM returns to DOS.
Use an explicit extension to distinguish DATE.CMD from DATE.EXE.

RSPs:
  PIN, CLOCK, PCTMP and CONFIDD are resident system services. There are
  no separate DIR, TYPE or other utility-command RSPs in this 4.1 build.
  DIR and CLS are shell built-ins. Explicit DIR.CMD is a separate tool.

Programs and help data are distributed across several disks. Mount the
disk containing the requested program; HELP does not install that program.
See the media README for complete disk contents.

///1COMSIZE
  COMSIZE  allows you to increase and decrease the amount of memory  allocated
  to the current window for programs with the COM file extension.  The default
  memory allocation is 128 kilobytes.

  Syntax:
  COMSIZE
  COMSIZE=n

  Explanation:
  The first form displays the current memory allocation for programs with  the
  COM  file  extension.  The  second  form allows you  to  change  the  memory
  allocation.   (N can represent any value from 0 through 999,  in kilobytes.)
  Changes made with  COMSIZE  affect  the current  window  only and remain in
  effect  until you turn off or  reset  the computer or run COMSIZE again.
  You can create a BATCH file (see the BATCH command) to run COMSIZE and load
  a frequently used program.

  Note:  the current COMSIZE setting is the amount of memory a .COM program
  will request when it loads.  If this amount of memory is not available
  the program will not be able to load.  Remember that using a .COM program
  with a large COMSIZE will limit the amount of memory available for programs
  in other windows.

///1conventions

               COMMAND CONVENTIONS

  The following special symbols define command syntax.

  {}   surrounds an optional item.
  |    separates alternative items in a command line.
  <cr> indicates a carriage return.
  ^    indicates the Control Key.
  n    substitute a number for n.
  s    substitute a string (group) of characters for s.
  o    substitute an option or option list for o.
  []   type square brackets to enclose an option list.
  ()   type parens to enclose a range of options within an option list.
  RW   Read-Write attribute - opposite of RO
  RO   Read-Only attribute - opposite of RW
  SYS  System attribute - opposite of DIR
  DIR  Directory attribute - opposite of SYS
   ... preceding element can be repeated as many times as desired.
  *    wildcard: replaces all or part of a filename and/or filetype.
  ?    wildcard: replaces any single character
       in the same position of a filename and/or filetype.

///1COPY
  This command copies one or more files between disk drives and other devices.

  Syntax:
  COPY {/option} source{/option} destination{/option}

  Explanation:
  The  source  can be a file in a disk drive directory  or  a  keyboard.  COPY
  leaves the source unaltered.  The destination can be a disk drive directory,
  a  computer,  a printer,  or a new file name.   Specify a drive or directory
  by entering  the drive  letter  followed  by  a colon.   If you wish to copy
  from   or  to  a subdirectory,   assign  the letter to the subdirectory with
  CHDIR before  the COPY  command is entered.   Printer and window devices are
  specified by  the names shown in Section 8 of the User's Guide.

  The   source   file specification must contain  the  complete  filename  and
  file extension.   The destination can be indicated simply by the drive or
  device number unless you want to change the fine name.  You can use the
  wildcard characters, * and ?, to specify a group of files.

  COPY does not recognize passwords in the file specifications.  If your files
  are password protected, you must use PIP.

///1COPYMENU
  COPYMENU  is used to copy one or more menus between  existing  menu  files.

  Syntax:
  COPYMENU
  COPYMENU -Ssource-filespec -Ddestination-filespec {-Mmenuname{, menuname...}}

  EXAMPLE:
  COPYMENU menu.dat newmenu.dat main

///1DATE
  DATE.CMD is the native CP/M command. DATE.EXE on the DOS disk is the
  separate DOS interface; use an explicit extension to select it.
  Short years 00..77 mean 2000..2077; 78..99 mean 1978..1999.

    Syntax:

    DATE
    DATE CONTINUOUS
    DATE date/time-specification
    DATE SET

    Explanation:

    The DATE  utility  allows  you  to set  the system  clock  to the
    correct date and time. It also allows you to display the date and
    time on the current virtual console.

    The date is  represented in  conventional  month-day-year  format
    MM/DD/YY  while the  time is represented in 24-hour clock format:
    00:00:00  to  11:59:59  indicates  AM,  and  12:00:00 to 23:59:59
    indicates PM.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>DATE

    Displays the current date and time.

    A>DATE C

    Displays the date and time continuously.

    A>DATE 11/28/83 10:35:45

    Sets the date and time as specified.

    A>DATE SET

    Prompts you for date and time entries.

///1dcomp
  Syntax:

  DCOMP drive1: {drive2:} {[1]}

  Purpose:

      DCOMP compares every sector of two floppy diskettes and reports
  differences by track and side. It compares raw disk contents, including
  the boot area and unused sectors; it does not interpret a filesystem.

  Arguments and options:

  drive1:   First installed floppy drive, A: or B: (required).
  drive2:   Second floppy drive; defaults to the current CP/M drive.
  [1]       Compare head zero only. The closing ] may be omitted.

      Specify the same drive twice to compare diskettes by swapping them
  at the prompts. The option may follow a drive directly, as in B:[1].
  Answer Y to compare another pair, or N to exit. An empty or invalid
  command line displays usage without starting disk operations.

      This build supports CP/M-86 1.1 using ROM BIOS and CCP/M-86 3.1
  using native IBM PC XIOS. Both support 40 cylinders with eight 512-byte
  sectors per head: 160K single-sided or 320K double-sided media. Close
  all open files first on CCP/M. Ctrl-C cancels and releases disk locks.

  Examples:

      A>DCOMP A: B:
      A>DCOMP A: A:[1]
      A>DCOMP A: B:[1

///1dcopy
  Syntax:

  DCOPY drive1: {drive2:} {[1]}

  Purpose:

      DCOPY copies an entire floppy diskette from drive1: onto drive2:,
  replacing the target contents. It copies raw sectors, including the
  boot area and unused sectors, and verifies the writes. It can format
  target tracks while copying when the original utility's retry logic
  detects that formatting is needed. No filesystem is interpreted.

  Arguments and options:

  drive1:   Source installed floppy drive, A: or B: (required).
  drive2:   Target floppy drive; defaults to the current CP/M drive.
  [1]       Copy head zero only. The closing ] may be omitted.

      Specify the same drive twice for a one-drive copy; insert source
  and target diskettes as prompted. The option may follow a drive
  directly, as in B:[1]. Answer Y to copy another pair, or N to exit.
  An empty or invalid command line displays usage without copying.

      This build supports CP/M-86 1.1 using ROM BIOS and CCP/M-86 3.1
  using native IBM PC XIOS. Both support 40 cylinders with eight 512-byte
  sectors per head: 160K single-sided or 320K double-sided media. Close
  all open files first on CCP/M. Ctrl-C cancels and releases disk locks.

  Examples:

      A>DCOPY A: B:
      A>DCOPY A: A:[1]
      A>DCOPY A: B:[1

///1DEL
DEL filespec
Alias for native ERA.CMD in both PCTMP and BATCH. Arguments are preserved.
Use ERA for its command reference. ERASE.EXE is a separate DOS executable.

///1DELQ
DELQ filespec
Alias for native ERAQ.CMD, which asks before deleting each matching file.

///1DIR
Built-in shell/BATCH syntax:
  DIR {filespec} {/L|/W} {/S} {/P}
  DIR {filespec} [L|W,S,P]

/L selects long output; /W selects wide output. /S displays system files
only. On DOS media hidden files are treated as system files too. /P
pauses after a screen page with Press any key to continue...
Without /S, system/hidden files are omitted. DOS defaults to long output;
CP/M defaults to wide output. DOS subdirectory names are marked with a
slash; CP/M user numbers apply only to CP/M media.

CP/M bracket spellings use the same flags: [S], [P], [L], [W], [S,P].
The closing ] may be omitted at the end: DIR B: [S
Slash and bracket forms can be combined. Options can precede or follow
the filespec. /S is a system-file filter, not recursive directory walking.

Examples:
  DIR B: /S
  DIR B: [S]
  DIR B: [S
  DIR B:*.CMD /L [P]

Explicit DIR.CMD uses the separately imported native command, with
its own CP/M [S], [Gn] and [P] options. Those are not a separate DIR RSP.
Use USER to change the CP/M user number for the built-in DIR.

///1DISKSTAT
DISKSTAT is the retained OEM disk-allocation display utility. Its menus
show file, directory and data-block allocation and allow drive selection.

///1DREDIX
DREDIX {filename}
Starts the DR EDIX full-screen text editor. Alt-H or F7 opens its help;
Alt-W writes the file and Alt-X exits. Ctrl-F/Ctrl-B move through lessons.

HELP.EDX supplies help screens; LSN.EDX supplies the interactive tutorial.
Both are packaged with DREDIX.CMD. Select that disk as the current drive:
the editor looks on the current drive, then A:, for its data files.
These files are separate from the system HELP.HLP database.

///1DSKMAINT
  DSKMAINT performs these diskette maintenance operations:   formatting new or
  reusable  diskettes,   copying   one  diskette's contents  to  another,  and
  verifying the integrity of the data on diskettes.

  Syntax:
  DSKMAINT

  Explanation:
  DSKMAINT  does not work if there are open files in any other  window.   When
  DSKMAINT is invoked, the menu displayed offers these options:

  F3  Verify a disk: Use this option to check a disk for unreadable sectors.
  F5  Copy  from  one  disk  to another:   Use this option  to  duplicate  the
      contents of one disk to another.
  F7  Format  disks:  Use  this  option to prepare new disks  for  use  or  to
      reformat used disks.

  Use  the Esc key to go back to the previous menu.   If a  verify,  copy,  or
  format operation is being performed,  Esc aborts the command and returns you
  to the previous menu or prompt.

  ///2DISKCOPY
  Press F5 to copy the contents of one disk to another.  Immediately after you
  select  the copy option,  DSKMAINT asks for the source drive containing  the
  disk  you  want  to copy.   Insert the source disk  first,  then  press  the
  corresponding  function  key  to  proceed.   Next,  DSKMAINT  asks  for  the
  destination drive containing the disk where the copy will be placed.  Insert
  the destination disk first, then press the corresponding function key.

  If  the source and destination disk formats match,  DSKMAINT summarizes  the
  assignments and displays the following prompt:

  This will erase data on the destination disk in drive A: or B:
  Is this what you want to do (Y or N)?

  If there are files on the destination disk,  they will be erased as the copy
  is  made.    To preserve the contents of the destination disk,  press N.  To
  proceed with the copy, press Y.

  If  the source and destination disks do not match,  DSKMAINT asks you  if
  you want to format the destination disk.   If you press N,  you are returned
  to  the Select Source Disk prompt.   (Be sure to insert a  destination  disk
  that is the same format as the source this time.)  If you enter Y,  DSKMAINT
  formats  the  entire destination disk to match the format of the source  and
  checks it.   Then DSKMAINT writes and checks the tracks read and repeats the
  read, write, and check cycle for all remaining tracks.

  When  copying disks in a single drive system,  the potential  for  confusing
  disks  and  copying  to  the wrong disk is high.   Be  sure  to  pay  strict
  attention  to  the DSKMAINT source and destination disk  swap  instructions.
  You are strongly advised to cover the write-protect notch on the source disk
  before beginning the copy so that DSKMAINT cannot write to the source disk.

  When  a copy error occurs,  DSKMAINT beeps and displays an error message  on
  the status line.  When this occurs, your options are to retry the operation,
  ignore the error, or accept the error.

  ///2FORMAT
  Press  F7 to prepare new disks for use or to reformat used disks.   You must
  format a disk before you can record files on it.  There are four options:

  1.  Format a single-sided disk with the DOS format.
  2.  Format a double-sided disk with the DOS format.
  3.  Format a single-sided disk with the CP/M format.
  4.  Format a double-sided disk with the CP/M format.

  Select  the  option  you want by pressing the  corresponding  function  key.
  DSKMAINT  responds  by  asking for the disk drive.   Insert the disk  to  be
  formatted  into a drive and press the function key assigned to  that  drive.
  Before  the  formatting operation begins,  you are reminded that  formatting
  destroys  existing  files.   If  you  choose to go  ahead  with  the  format
  operation anyway,  all tracks are formatted and subsequently checked. If the
  disk fails the check a second time,  discard the disk.   After a  successful
  check,  DSKMAINT returns you to the list of format options to format another
  disk.  To exit from DSKMAINT, press the Esc key twice.

///1DSP
DSP displays print-spool information. It is supplied with PRINTMGR and
the OEM SPL spooler support, including INDEX.DSP. PRINT is a separate
direct-printing utility imported from CCP/M.

///1ED
    Syntax:

    ED input-filespec {d:|output-filespec}

    Explanation:

    ED is the Concurrent CP/M  character file editor.  You can rename
    or redirect the  new  version  of  the  file  by  specifying  the
    destination drive or destination filespec.

    Section 10  of the  Concurrent  CP/M  User's  Reference  Guide is
    devoted to ED.

    ///2COMMANDS
    ED Command Summary:

    Note: CP points to the current character in the edit buffer.

    nA            append n lines from original file to memory buffer.
    0A            append file until buffer is one-half full
    #A            append file until buffer is full (or end of file)
    B, -B         move CP to the beginning (B) or bottom (-B) of buffer
    nC, -nC       move CP n characters forward (C) or back (-C) through buffer
    nD, -nD       delete n characters before (-D) or from (D) the CP
    E             save new file and return to Concurrent CP/M
    Fstring^Z     find character string
    H             save new file, re-edit using new file as the original file
    I<RETURN>     enter insert mode
    Istring^Z     insert string at CP
    Jsearch_str^Zins_str^Zdel_to_str     juxtapose strings
    nK, -nK       delete (kill) n lines from the CP
    nL, -nL       move CP n lines
    nMcommand     execute command n times
    n, -n         move CP n lines and display that line
    n:            move to line n
    :ncommand     execute command through line n
    Nstring       extended find string
    O             return to original file
    nP, -nP       move CP n lines forward and display n lines at console
    Q             abandon new file, return to Concurrent CP/M
    R             read X$$$$$$$.LIB file into buffer
    Rfilespec^Z   read filespec into buffer
    Sdelete string^Zinsert string^Z     substitute string
    nT, -nT       type n lines
    U, -U         upper-case translation
    V, -V         line numbering on/off
    0V            display free buffer space
    nW            write n lines to new file
    0W            write until buffer is half empty
    nX            write or append n lines to X$$$$$$$.LIB
    nXfilespec^Z  write n lines to filespec or append if previous X
                  command applied to the same file
    0X            delete file X$$$$$$$.LIB
    0Xfilespec^Z  delete filespec
    nZ            wait n seconds

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>ED TEST.DAT
    A>ED TEST.DAT B:
    A>ED TEST.DAT TEST2.DAT
    A>ED TEST.DAT B:TEST2.DAT

    Command Examples:

    :#a
    :p
    :4c0tt
    :e
    :5K
    :B5T
    :Rmyfile.txt

///1EDITMENU
  EDITMENU  is  used  to create a new menu or to  edit,  copy,  or  delete  an
  existing menu.

  Syntax:
  EDITMENU
  EDITMENU filespec

  Explanation:
  The first form invokes the default menu file, MENU.DAT, which you can use to
  create,  edit,  copy, or delete menus.  The second form allows you to name a
  new  menu  file or to access an existing menu file.   You can  then  create,
  edit, copy, or delete menus in the specified menu file.

  EXAMPLES:
  EDITMENU
  EDITMENU mymenus.dat

///1ERA
    Syntax:

    ERA filespec

    Explanation:

    ERA erases a file or group of files.  ERA accepts ambiguous  file
    specifications.  The ERAQ command works like ERA, but prompts you
    before performing the erasure.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>ERA DRAFT.BAK
    A>ERA B:LETTER.DAT
    A>ERA C:LETTER.*
    A>ERA D:*.BAK
    A>ERA B:*.*

///1ERAQ
    Syntax:

    ERAQ filespec
    ERAQ filespec [XFCB]

    Explanation:

    ERAQ erases a file or group of files, prompting you  before  each
    deletion.  ERAQ accepts ambiguous filenames.    The [XFCB] option
    erases only the extra directory information for passwords.

    The ERA command works as ERAQ  does,  but  does  not  prompt  you
    before performing the erasure.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    1A>ERAQ B:*.CMD

    B:EXAMPLE    CMD ?y
    B:FUNCTION   CMD ?n
    B:SDIR       CMD ?y
    B:PIP        CMD ?y
    1A>

    In the example, the user instructs ERAQ to delete all  the  files
    except C:FUNCTION.CMD.

    2B>ERAQ myfile.dat [XFCB]

    The command line above erases only the password information for the
    file myfile.dat.  First use SET to turn off password protection.

///1ERASE
  Syntax:
  ERASE filespec or DEL filespec

  Explanation:
  ERASE   erases   one  or  more   files.    ERASE  accepts  ambiguous    file
  specifications.   The  ERAQ command is like ERASE,   but prompts you  before
  performing the erasure.

  ///2EXAMPLES
  Examples:
  A>ERASE DRAFT.BAK
  A>ERASE C:LETTER.*
  A>ERASE D:*.BAK
  A>ERASE B:*.*

///1FILESPEC
    Concurrent CP/M  identifies   every  file   by  its  unique  file
    specification.  The  term  "filespec" is an abbreviation for file
    specification. A filespec can have four parts:

                      d:filename.typ;password

    d:         represents the optional drive specification, which can
               range from A through P, followed by a colon.

    filename   represents the required filename, which can be 1 to  8
               alphabetic or numeric characters.

    .typ       represents the optional filetype,  which can be 1 to 3
               alphabetic or numeric characters preceded by a period.

    ;password  represents the optional password,  which can be 1 to 8
               alphabetic  or  numeric  characters,   preceded  by  a
               semicolon.

    Valid combinations of the elements of a  file  specification  are
    shown below:

      filename
      d:filename
      filename.typ
      d:filename.typ
      filename;password
      d:filename;password
      filename.typ;password
      d:filename.typ;password

    Certain Concurrent CP/M commands select and process several files
    if  "wildcard" characters appear  in  the  filename  or filetype.
    The two wildcard characters are  ?, which matches a single letter
    in the same  position, and *, which matches any character at that
    position  and  any  other characters remaining in the filename or
    filetype.

    ///2CONVENTIONS

               COMMAND CONVENTIONS

  The following special symbols define command syntax.

  {}   surrounds an optional item.
  |    separates alternative items in a command line.
  <cr> indicates a carriage return.
  ^    indicates the Control Key.
  n    substitute a number for n.
  s    substitute a string (group) of characters for s.
  o    substitute an option or option list for o.
  []   type square brackets to enclose an option list.
  ()   type parens to enclose a range of options within an option list.
  RW   Read-Write attribute - opposite of RO
  RO   Read-Only attribute - opposite of RW
  SYS  System attribute - opposite of DIR
  DIR  Directory attribute - opposite of SYS
   ... preceding element can be repeated as many times as desired.
  *    wildcard: replaces all or part of a filename and/or filetype.
  ?    wildcard: replaces any single character
       in the same position of a filename and/or filetype.

///1FM
FM
Starts the retained OEM file manager. Use its on-screen menus for file
operations. FM.CFG is supplied beside it on the OEM utilities disk.

///1FREEMEMORY
SYSTAT [M] displays memory partitions; SYSTAT [O] displays system limits
and free descriptor/queue resources. SHOW reports disk space, including
the memory disk; it does not report free program memory.

///1FUNCTION
  The  FUNCTION  command allows you to reassign the function keys and the keys
  used to switch windows.

  Syntax:
  FUNCTION
  FUNCTION {d:}filename.PFK

  Explanation:
  When you enter FUNCTION without a file specification,  Concurrent displays a
  menu  presenting  you with options.   When you enter FUNCTION  with  a  file
  specification,  function  assignments are made from the file specified.   No
  menu is displayed and no user interaction is necessary.

  Function key assignments generally apply only to command line entries.  Many
  Concurrent  commands and application programs use function keys for  special
  purposes and will ignore any assignments you make with the FUNCTION command.

  When you exit some application programs,   however, the function keys do not
  return  to their original assignment.    You should,  therefore,  save  your
  default  key assignments  in  a file and run FUNCTION with that file if  you
  need to restore the function key settings.

  The following table describes the options presented when you invoke FUNCTION
  without a file specification.

      Selection                     Description

      F3  Assign Function Keys:   Select this option to display or change  the
          current  function  key assignments for those commands you  use  most
          often.

      F5  Save  Function  Keys  in a File:   Select this options to  save  new
          function key assignments in a file.

      F7  Change Window Switching Keys:  Select this option to change the  key
          combination used to switch windows.

  All  assignments with FUNCTION take effect as soon as you exit the  FUNCTION
  command.   They  remain  in  effect only until you turn off  or  reset  your
  computer,  however.   If you want to save your assignments, select option F5
  before exiting FUNCTION.  If you want these settings to take effect when you
  start Concurrent, use the Save System Parameters option of SETUP.

  Press  Esc  to  return to the previous menu.   Also,  use Esc  to  exit  the
  FUNCTION main menu and return to the command line prompt.

  F3  Assign Function Keys

  The  Concurrent commands shown for functions F1 through F10 are the  default
  commands,  when  you select F3.   To change the command assigned,  press the
  particular  function  key.   FUNCTION  then highlights  the  number  of  the
  function  key selected and erases the currently assigned command.   You  can
  now enter a new command.

  To  make an entry,  type the command exactly as if you were entering it from
  the  command line.   Use the backspace key to edit the command as you  enter
  it.   When you are satisfied with the command,  press Ctrl @ or enter \00 to
  conclude the entry.  You can then select another function key or exit.

  The maximum length for each assignment is 20 characters long.

  If  you  want Concurrent to run a command when you press  the  corresponding
  function key, press Enter before terminating the command entry.  If you want
  Concurrent to wait for an additional entry,  do not place an <Enter> at  the
  end of your command.   For example, if you wanted a function key to give you
  an alphabetical listing of the files on the current drive,  you would assign
  the following command to the key:

  SDIR <Enter>

  If,  on  the other hand,  you wanted Concurrent to wait and let you  specify
  which drive to list, you would make the following assignment instead.

  SDIR

  Afterwards, when you press the associated function key, Concurrent waits for
  you  to  press  Enter  before  running the  command.   This  gives  you  the
  opportunity to enter a drive specification or other option.

  Note:   When  you press the spacebar during  function  assignment,  FUNCTION
  displays \20 instead of a literal space.   When you press Enter,  Concurrent
  displays \OD.   These are the numerical (hexadecimal) representations of the
  spacebar and the Enter key.

  When  you  finish  making assignments to the F1 through F10  function  keys,
  press  Enter  to  proceed to the second page of  the  assign  function  keys
  display,   which  shows  the  numeric  keypad  and  Alt  plus  function  key
  assignments.  Press Enter again to proceed to the third and last page of the
  display,  which  shows  Shift plus function key and Ctrl plus  function  key
  assignments.

  Make  assignments to any of these keys the same way as for function keys  F1
  through F10.  The maximum length for each assignment is:

         Function keys in combination with the Ctrl,  Alt,  and Shift keys:  4
         characters long

         Numeric keypad keys: 4 characters long (the 5 cannot be reassigned)

  Press  Enter to cycle through the display quickly.   Press Esc when you  are
  finished making your assignments to return to the FUNCTION main menu.

  F5  Save Function Keys in a File

  After  you  have made your command assignments and returned to the  FUNCTION
  main  menu,  press F5 to record your assignments.   FUNCTION displays a  new
  menu presenting you with the following options:

       Press F3 to save the assignments under a filename of your choice.   The
       extension is also PFK.

       Press F5 to save the assignments under the default filename, KEYS.PFK.

  When you select F3,  FUNCTION erases the KEYS filename and waits for you  to
  enter a new one.   The PFK file extension remains on the screen and there is
  no  cursor.   Enter any  valid filename, up to a maximum of eight characters.

  F7  Reset Window Switching Keys (See HELP topic, FUNCTION SWITCH.)

  ///2SAVE
  FUNCTION  allows  you  to assign commands to function keys for  push  button
  execution  of commonly used commands.   You must use the Save  Function  Key
  Assignments  if you want Concurrent to remember these settings each time you
  use Concurrent.   Concurrent can store the function key settings in a  file.
  You  can  restore the function key settings by typing:  FUNCTION  <filespec>
  where <filespec> is the name of function key file.

  The default file name is KEYS.PFK;  however, you can change the file name to
  have  any name you choose.   It is often useful to have  different  function
  keys  files  for different applications.   By changing the file name  before
  saving the file, you can create another function key file.

  Press  function  key F3 if you want to change the name of the  function  key
  file.   Press  function  key  F5 to save the function key  settings  in  the
  displayed filename.

  ///2SWITCH
  This option lets you change the keys used to switch windows.  This is needed
  when  an application you are running uses the defined window switching  keys
  for  another  purpose.   The entire range of combinations is shown  on  your
  screen  when you press F7.   The current key combination is indicated by the
  flashing arrow.

  Notice that option F2 allows you to select a window by pressing the  numeric
  keypad  key only.   All other options require the use of the numeric  keypad
  key in combination with one or more other keys.

  To  select  an  option,  press the associated function key  and  Esc.   This
  returns  you  to the FUNCTION main menu.   Function key F5 also  saves  your
  window switching key selection.  (Refer to HELP topic, FUNCTION.)

///1GENCCPM
GENCCPM is the native system-generation utility. It combines system CON
modules and selected RSPs, applies configuration settings and produces
CCPM.SYS. Follow its configuration prompts. The project build supplies a
prepared configuration and module set; see the kernel/sysgen directory.

///1gencmd
    Syntax:

    GENCMD filespec {options}

    Purpose:

    Converts hexadecimal object file (filetype  assumed  to  be .H86)
    into  executable  file  (of  type .CMD). Switches controlling the
    type of memory model and group addresses can  be  included.   All
    values are hexadecimal and are paragraph values.

    ///2Examples
    Examples:

        A>GENCMD PROG
        A>GENCMD PROG1 8080
        A>GENCMD PROG2 DATA[M20]
        A>GENCMD PROG3 DATA[B4C,M260,XFFF]
        A>GENCMD PROG4 CODE[A40] DATA[M30]

    ///2options
    Syntax:

    GENCMD filespec  {8080
                      CODE[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                      DATA[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                     STACK[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                     EXTRA[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]}

       A = Absolute memory location
       B = Beginning address of group in .H86 file
       M = Minimum memory required
       X = Maximum memory required

    All values represented by n are hexadecimal paragraph addresses.

///1GENLDR
GENLDR is the original CDOS loader-generation utility. It is supplied on
the tools disk with LOADCCPM.COM. It generates loader material from the
system image; follow its prompts and the accompanying OEM documentation.

///1GET
    Syntax:

    GET [infile{,E|EI}] command-line

    Explanation:

    GET feeds the console input of a program from a text file instead
    of the keyboard.  The infile is read in 128-byte records until a
    CTRL-Z or the end of the file.  The line read at that point is
    empty, which ends programs such as PIP; any later input comes from
    the keyboard.  The program and any programs it starts on this
    virtual console receive the characters; other consoles are not
    affected.

    E or EI echoes the characters supplied from the file to the screen.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected; use
    the program forms such as SDIR.CMD or TYPE.CMD.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>GET [SCRIPT.IN] ED MYFILE.TXT
    A>GET [CMDS.DAT] PIP

    See also: PUT, RDR

///1HDMAINT
HDMAINT
The retained OEM hard-disk maintenance utility. Use its menus for the
supported partition/format/system-disk operations. This is the CDOS 4.1
program, not the CP/M-86 HDMAINT 1.1N rebuild. Its source build remains
pending the original flat LBDOS loader; the original binary is packaged.
The original detailed operating reference is retained in base/help.hlp.

///1HELP

  Syntax:

     HELP {topic} {subtopic1 ... subtopic8} {[NOPAGE|LIST]}

  Explanation:

  HELP  displays  a  list  of  topics   and   provides   summarized
  information for CCP/M commands.

  HELP topic displays information about that topic.
  HELP topic subtopic displays information about that subtopic.

  One or two letters is enough to identify the topics.  After  HELP
  displays   information   for   your  topic,   it   displays   the
  special  prompt  HELP> on your screen,  followed  by  a  list  of
  subtopics.

   -  Enter ? to display list of main topics.
   -  Enter a period and subtopic name to access subtopics.
   -  Enter a period to redisplay what you just read.
   -  Press the RETURN key to return to the CCP/M  system prompt.
   -  [NOPAGE] doesn't stop at 24 lines; console display keeps scrolling.
   -  [LIST] removes extra lines between headings; use with CTRL-P to print.
   -  Press any key to exit a display and return to the HELP> prompt.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

       A>HELP
       A>HELP DATE
       A>HELP DIR
       A>HELP>.EXAMPLES
       A>HELP DIR EXAMPLES
       HELP>SET
       HELP>.PASSWORD
       HELP>SET PASSWORD
       HELP>.
       HELP><cr>

///1HEXCOM
HEXCOM converts Intel HEX input to a flat COM binary. It is supplied as a
development tool alongside ASM80 and ASM86. Use GENCMD, not HEXCOM, to
package native CP/M-86 segmented H86 output into CMD format.

///1INITDIR
    Syntax:

    INITDIR d:

    Explanation:

    INITDIR  initializes   a   directory  to   allow  time  and  date
    stamping on a diskette or hard disk.  Attempting to  enable  time
    and date stamping on a directory that has not been reformatted by
    INITDIR  will  result in an error message.  INITDIR only works on
    disks that have already been formatted according to your computer
    manufacturer's instructions.

    ///2EXAMPLE
    Example:

    A>INITDIR C:

    INITDIR WILL ACTIVATE TIME STAMPS FOR SPECIFIED DRIVE
    Do you want to re-format the directory on drive: C (Y/N)?

    Answer with a "Y" to continue.  If the specified disk was already
    formatted for time and date stamps, INITDIR displays the message:

    Directory already re-formatted
    Do you want to recover time/date space (Y/N)?

    If your directory  is  full and  you previously  enabled time and
    date stamps, you can use INITDIR to free up the  directory space.
    If INITDIR  finds  time and  date  stamps  in  the  directory, it
    displays the following message:

    Do you want the existing time and date stamps cleared (Y/N)?

///1LOADCCPM
  LOADCCPM allows you to use Concurrent on a hard disk-based computer if  your
  hard disk is formatted only for DOS.

  Syntax:
  LOADCCPM

  Explanation:
  LOADCCPM  allows  you to use Concurrent even if your hard disk is  formatted
  only for DOS.   This is significant if you do not want to reformat your hard
  disk  when  you  buy Concurrent.   To use Concurrent,  make  sure  that  the
  CCPM.SYS  and  LOADCCPM.COM  files are on your  DOS  hard  disk.   To  start
  Concurrent,  first  start DOS.   At the DOS command prompt (e.g., C>),  type
  LOADCCPM  and  press  Enter.   You might find it  convenient  to  place  the
  LOADCCPM   in   your  DOS  AUTOEXEC.BAT  file  so  that  Concurrent   starts
  automatically when your computer is started.

///1MD
MD is a shell/BATCH alias for MKDIR.CMD. See MKDIR for syntax.

///1MEMORYDISK
  See SETUP MDISK.

///1MKDIR
  The MKDIR command makes subdirectories on DOS media.

  Syntax:
  MKDIR {d:} {\} {dirname} dirname

  Explanation:
  Use MKDIR to create a subdirectory.  Newly formatted DOS disks and hard disk
  partitions have one directory called the root directory.

  Subdirectories offer the same file storage facilities as the root directory.
  Programs  invoked from a subdirectory run no differently than they  do  when
  invoked from the root.   Subdirectories are useful for organizing files into
  groups.

  The path of subdirectories leading to the new one must be entered explicitly
  and  all subdirectories in the path must already exist.   For  example,  the
  following  command  creates  subdirectory  LEVEL4 on drive  B  only  if  the
  subdirectories LEVEL2 and LEVEL3 already exist:

  A>MKDIR B:LEVEL2\LEVEL3\LEVEL4

  If  a  subdirectory  is specified that does not already exist  in  the  path
  leading to the new directory,  Concurrent displays the following message and
  terminates the command:

  Directory missing

  A  subdirectory  specification,  like a file specification,  consists of  an
  eight-character name and an optional three-character extension.   MKDIR  can
  be abbreviated as MD in your command line.

  When  you  enter a MKDIR command with multiple subdirectory  specifications,
  you must precede each specification except the first with a blackslash  (\).

///1N
N is the OEM national setup utility for the IBM PC. It uses NATIONAL.MSG
and the national tables in CCPM.SYS. Run it from its supplied OEM disk and
follow its language/setup prompts.

///1ORDER
  ORDER displays or changes the file extension search order used by Concurrent
  to find a program.

  Syntax:
  ORDER
  ORDER=ext1{,ext2{,ext3{,ext4}}}

  Explanation:
  When  you  enter a command,  Concurrent looks for the corresponding  program
  file and runs it.   Because there might be programs with identical filenames
  but  different file extensions,  Concurrent selects one file in  a  specific
  sequence of file extensions.  The default file extension search sequence is:
  CMD,  COM,  EXE, and BAT.  To have Concurrent run the BAT file first without
  having to specifically request COPY.BAT, you must change the search order.

  To display the current file extension search sequence, type:

  A>ORDER

  Separate  each file extension with a comma.   Omit those file extensions you
  do  not want Concurrent to look for.   If you want Concurrent to search  for
  BATCH files, first type:

  A>ORDER=BAT,CMD,COM,EXE

  Generally,   list  BAT  first  if  you  create  BATCH  files  for  all  your
  applications.

  A>ORDER=EXE,BAT

  The  preceding command makes Concurrent first look for an EXE file and  then
  the  BAT version.   If the search is unsuccessful,  Concurrent displays  the
  following message:

  Concurrent Error:  Can't Find Command

  You can request a CMD file by specifying the entire name: SDIR.CMD.

///1PATH
  See the SYSDISK command.

///1PAUSE
PAUSE
Displays Press any key to continue... and waits for a key. Implemented
by the shell and BATCH; no PAUSE.CMD is needed for this built-in.

///1PIP
  Syntax:
  PIP {d:{[Gn]}}|filespec{[Gn]} = filespec{[o]},... | d:{[o]}

  Explanation:
  The file copy program PIP copies files, combines files, and transfers  files
  between  disks,  printers,  consoles,  or  other  devices attached  to  your
  computer.  The first filespec is the destination. The second filespec is the
  source.  Use two or more source filespecs separated by commas to combine two
  or  more  files  into one file.   [o] is any combination  of  the  available
  options.   The  [Gn] option in the destination filespec tells PIP   to  copy
  your file to that user number.

  PIP  with  no command tail displays an * prompt and awaits  your  series  of
  commands,  entered  and  processed  one  line at  a  time.   The  source  or
  destination can be any Concurrent logical device.

  ///2Examples
  COPY A FILE FROM ONE DISK TO ANOTHER

       A>PIP b:=a:draft.txt
       A>PIP b:draft.txt = a:
       B3>PIP myfile.dat=A:[G9]
       A9>PIP B:[G3]=myfile.dat

  COPY A FILE AND RENAME IT

       A5>PIP newdraft.txt=oldraft.txt
       C8>PIP b:newdraft.txt=a:oldraft.txt

  COPY MULTIPLE FILES

       A>PIP b:=draft.*
       A>PIP b:=*.*
       B>PIP b:=c:.*.*
       C>PIP b:=*.txt[g5]
       C>PIP a:=*.cmd[wr]
       B>PIP a:[g3]=c:*.*

  COMBINE MULTIPLE FILES

       A>PIP b:new.dat=file1.dat,file2.dat

  COPY, RENAME AND PLACE IN USER 1

       A>pip newdraft.txt[g1]=oldraft.txt

  COPY, RENAME AND GET FROM USER 1

       A>PIP newdraft.txt=oldraft.txt[g1]

  COPY TO/FROM LOGICAL DEVICES

       A>PIP b:funfile.sue=con:
       A>PIP lst:=con:
       A>PIP lst:=b:draft.txt[t8]
       A>PIP prn:=b:draft.txt

  ///2options
  PIP OPTIONS

  A    Archive.  Copy only files that have been changed since the last copy.
  C    Confirm. PIP prompts for confirmation before each file copy.
  Dn   Delete any characters past column n.
  E    Echo transfer to window.
  F    Filter form-feeds from source data.
  Gn   Get from or go to user n.
  H    Test for valid hex format.
  I    Ignore :00 hex data records and test for valid hex format.
  K    Kill display of filespecs on window.
  L    Translate uppercase to lowercase.
  N    Number output lines.
  O    Object file transfer, ^Z ignored.
  Pn   Set page length to n (default n=60).
  Qs   Quit copying from source at string s.
  R    Read files that have been set to SYStem.
  Ss   Start copying from the source at the string s.
  Tn   Expand tabs to n spaces.
  U    Translate lowercase to uppercase.
  V    Verify that data has been written correctly.
  W    Write over Read/Only files without window query.
  Z    Zero the parity bit.

  All options except C,G,K,O,R,V and W force an ASCII file transfer, character
  by character, terminated by a ^Z.

///1PRINT
    Syntax:

    PRINT filename{[options]}{,filename{[options],...}

    Explanation:

    The  PRINT  utility  allows  you to send the files you enter on a
    PRINT command line to the printer.

    You must enter at least one filespec on a PRINT command line. You
    can enter as many filespecs as will fit on Concurrent CP/M's 128-
    character  command  line.   PRINT  allows  you  to  use  wildcard
    filespecs.   On a PRINT command line, separate filespecs with one
    space or a comma.

    PRINT Options

    o S (SYSTEM) - The S option  allows you to  print a file that has
      the SYS attribute.

    o Tn (TAB) - n is  a  number  of character spaces.   The T option
      allows you to expand or compress tab characters. PRINT replaces
      each  tab in a file  with  enough  spaces  to position the next
      character in a column that is divisible by n.   PRINT's default
      setting for tab expansion is eight spaces.

    o Gn (GET FROM) - n is a user number.  The G option allows you to
      print a file that is not in the current user number.

    Enter options in square brackets following the filespec for which
    you want to exercise the option.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    To print more than one copy of a file, repeat the file's filespec
    on the PRINT command line for each additional copy that you want.

    4B>print murphy.txt[g3],murphy.txt[g3],murphy.txt[g3]

    This example prints three copies of the file MURPHY.TXT in user 3
    of drive B.

    2B>print *.txt,example.ltr[g0],a:backup.sub[g0s]

    In the above example,  PRINT  prints all the files in the current
    directory with a filetype of TXT, the file EXAMPLE.LTR in user 0,
    and the SUBMIT file  BACKUP.SUB,  which has the SYS attribute and
    is stored in user 0 of drive A.

    10A>print abacus.del[g3],*.vrf[t10] angst.sub[sg0] milton.dun

    The above  command causes  PRINT to print the file  ABACUS.DEL in
    user 3,  and all files  with a  filetype  of  VRF in  the current
    directory.   PRINT  expands the tabs in these files to 10 spaces.
    PRINT then continues with  ANGST.SUB,  a SUBMIT file with the SYS
    attribute in user 0, and finishes with the file MILTON.DUN.  Note
    that commas or spaces are acceptable in separating files.

    B>print *.*

    The above command causes PRINT to print all of the files with the
    DIR attribute in user 0 of drive B.

///1PRINTER
    Syntax:

    PRINTER {n}

    Explanation:

    PRINTER displays or selects the printer device  attached  to  the
    current  virtual  console. Several  consoles  can  share the same
    printer, but only one process can use a given printer at a  time.
    When  you  enter the PRINTER command without a number, the system
    returns the number of the printer assigned to the current virtual
    console. To  select a printer, enter the PRINTER command followed
    by the  printer  number  to  be  attached.  Note:  Refer  to  the
    instruction  manual  supplied  by  your  hardware manufacturer to
    determine how many printers your system supports.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>PRINTER            ; displays the current printer number
    Printer Number = 1

    A>PRINTER 3          ; sets the current printer number
    Printer Number = 3

///1PRINTMGR
  PRINTMGR allows you to print one or more files without tying up a window.

  Syntax:
  PRINTMGR command

  Explanation:
  PRINTMGR  can be used from the command line or from a menu if your  computer
  has  a hard disk.   To use the menu form of PRINTMGR,  press function key F6
  from  the  Concurrent Startup Menu.   Follow the on-screen  menus  and  help
  screens  to  use  PRINTMGR.   The rest of this discussion applies  to  using
  PRINTMGR from the command line.

  PRINTMGR must be started before you can use it.  From the command line, type
  PRINTMGR  START  n  n ...  where n is (are) the  printer  number(s)  of  the
  printers you want PRINTMGR to use.  For example, if you want PRINTMGR to use
  printer 0, type PRINTMGR START 0.

  Once PRINTMGR is started,  you can use it to print one or more files.   From
  the command line, type
  PRINTMGR  file file ...  where file is (are) the name(s) of the file(s)  you
  want  to  print.   There are several options you can specify when you  print
  files--see the Concurrent User's Guide section on printer manager.

  Other PRINTMGR commands include:
  PRINTMGR STATUS     lists all the print jobs and their status.
  PRINTMGR DELETE     deletes a print job.
  PRINTMGR RESET      stops the printer manager and deletes all print jobs.
  PRINTMGR TERMINATE  suspends the printer manager until its restarted.
  PRINTMGR HELP       prints a command summary.

  See the Concurrent User's Guide for more information on these commands.

///1PUT
    Syntax:

    PUT [outfile{,A}{,E|EO}] command-line

    Explanation:

    PUT writes everything a program sends to the console (BDOS
    functions 2, 6 and 9) to a disk file instead of showing it.  The
    program and any programs it starts on this virtual console are
    captured; other consoles are not affected.  An existing outfile is
    replaced.

    A       appends to outfile instead of replacing it.
    E or EO also shows the output on the screen.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected; use
    the program forms such as SDIR.CMD or TYPE.CMD.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>PUT [SDIR.TXT] SDIR.CMD
    A>PUT [BUILD.LOG,A] LINK86 MYPROG
    A>PUT [BUILD.LOG,A,EO] LIB86 MYLIB

    See also: GET, RDR

///1RD
RD is a shell/BATCH alias for RMDIR.CMD. See RMDIR for syntax.

///1RDR
    Syntax:

    RDR [infile,outfile{,A}{,E|EI|EO}] command-line

    Explanation:

    RDR redirects the console input and the console output of a program
    at the same time: input is read from infile (as with GET) and
    output is written to outfile (as with PUT).

    A       appends to outfile instead of replacing it.
    EI      echoes the input read from infile to the screen.
    EO      echoes the output written to outfile to the screen.
    E       echoes both.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>RDR [SCRIPT.IN,BUILD.LOG] ED MYFILE.TXT
    A>RDR [COMMANDS.DAT,PIP.LOG,EO] PIP
    A>RDR [NEXTCMD.IN,MASTER.LOG,A,EO] PIP

    See also: GET, PUT

///1REDOS
  REDOS is used to make DOS self-starting from your hard disk.

  Syntax:
  REDOS {d:}

  Explanation:
  REDOS  is  used to make DOS self-starting from your hard disk if  your  hard
  disk  had been set up to start Concurrent.   To do this,  start DOS on  your
  computer.   At the DOS prompt (e.g.  C>),  type REDOS and then the hard disk
  drive  letter  followed by a :  and then Enter.   REDOS will prompt  you  to
  insert your DOS system disk in drive A.

  ///2EXAMPLE
  REDOS C: <Enter>

  The above command,  when entered while running DOS,  will initiate an action
  that will cause DOS to start from drive C.

///1REM
REM text
Ignores the rest of the command line, in the shell and BATCH.
A line beginning with a semicolon or period is also a comment.

///1REN
    Syntax:

    REN {d:}newname{.typ}=oldname{.typ}

    Explanation:

    REN changes the name of the existing file (specified by  oldname)
    to  a  new  name  (specified  by newname). You cannot specify two
    different drives.  If  the  file  specified by newname is already
    present in  the directory,  REN displays the following message on
    the screen:

    Not renamed: filename.ext already exists, delete (Y/N)?

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>REN NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT
    A>REN B:NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT
    A>REN B:NEWLIST=OLDLIST
    A>REN NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT

    Not renamed: NEWFILE.DAT already exists, delete (Y/N)? Y
    NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT

    In the last example, the new filename already  exists.  The  user
    overrides the error by entering a Y at the prompt.

///1RENAME
  See REN.

///1REST
  See the BACKREST command.

///1RMDIR
  The RMDIR command removes a specified empty subdirectory.

  Syntax:
  RMDIR {d:} {\}subspec{\subspec\subspec ... }

  Explanation:
  You  cannot remove a subdirectory that contains files.   Use PIP or COPY  to
  copy  files to another directory and then ERASE them from  the  subdirectory
  you want to remove.  You can remove a subdirectory from the current drive or
  from  a  designated drive;  however,  you cannot remove the root or  current
  directory.

  The subdirectory to be removed must be preceded by the current  subdirectory
  and   any  subdirectories  leading  to  it.    Separate  each   subdirectory
  specification  with a backslash (\).   For example,  this command erases the
  subdirectory SUB3 on drive A:

  A>RMDIR SUB1\SUB2\SUB3

///1RUNMENU
  RUNMENU allows you to run a menu.

  Syntax:
  RUNMENU
  RUNMENU filespec
  RUNMENU filespec menuname

  Explanation:
  If  you  do not specify a menu file,  the  default,  MENU.DAT,  is  assumed.
  Replace filespec in the command line with the name of the menu file you want
  RUNMENU to access.  Replace menuname with the name of a specific menu within
  that menu file.   RUNMENU then runs the specified menu.   The menu file  you
  specify must be in your current directory.

///1SDIR
    Syntax:

    SDIR {d:}{filespec}{,filespec}{[option]|[option=modifier]}

    The SDIR utility is an enhanced version of the DIR utility.  SDIR
    can  search  for  files  on any or all drives, in any or all user
    areas. Only one option list is allowed. The most efficient way to
    become  familiar with SDIR is to use it. SDIR does not change any
    information on diskette or in memory, so you can experiment  with
    it freely.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>SDIR [xfcb] D:*.CMD
    A>SDIR [nosort,ro] A: B: C:
    A>SDIR [user=3,exclude] *.CMD
    A>SDIR [size,rw] D:
    A>SDIR B: [PAGE]
    A>SDIR [PAGE,FULL] A: B:
    A>SDIR [user=all,drive=all,sys] *.PLI *.CMD *.A86

    ///2OPTIONS
    Options:

    [DRIVE=d]     Displays files on the specified drive only.
    [DRIVE=all]   Displays files on all online drives.
    [EXCLUDE]     Displays only files that do not match filespec.
    [FF]          Prints a Form Feed character at the start of each header.
    [FULL]        The default SDIR option.
    [SHORT]       Displays names only, with a backslash for DOS directories.
    [HELP]        Displays a compact summary of the supported options.
    [LENGTH=n]    Displays a new (Page) header every n lines.
    [MESSAGE]     Causes SDIR to display where it is searching for files.
    [SORT]        Sorts names and types (the default).
    [ATTRIBUTES]  Displays archive and user-defined attribute bits.
    [DATE]        Displays timestamps where timestamp information exists.
    [NOSORT]      Displays files in the order that they are found on the disk.
    [PAGE]        Pauses after 23 screen rows, including headers and wraps.
    [NOPAGE]      Disables pauses (the default).
    [RO]          Displays Read-Only files only.
    [RW]          Displays Read/Write attribute files only.
    [SIZE]        Displays only file name and size.
    [SYS]         Displays SYStem attribute files only.
    [DIR]         Displays non-system files (not a subdirectory-only filter).
    [USER=n]      Displays files on user n only.
    [USER=all]    Displays files on all user numbers.
    [XFCB]        Displays only files with XFCBs or date stamping.
    [NONXFCB]     Displays only files without XFCBs or date stamping.

    DOS subdirectories display <DIR> instead of a file size in FULL/SIZE;
    SHORT/SIZE prefix directory names with a backslash. Directory counts
    are separate from regular files. Hidden entries remain visible.

    [PAGE] uses the native PCTMP message: Press any key to continue...
    Press a key for the next page, or Ctrl-C to stop. [LENGTH=n] controls
    repeated headers separately; it does not enable interactive pauses.

///1SET
SET filespec [RO|RW,DIR|SYS,ARCHIVE=ON|OFF,F1=ON|OFF,...,F4=ON|OFF]
SET filespec[;password] [PASSWORD=newpassword,PROTECT=READ|WRITE|DELETE|NONE]
SET {d:} [NAME=label.typ]
SET {d:} [RO|RW]
SET {d:} [PROTECT=ON|OFF]
SET {d:} [DEFAULT=password]
SET {d:} [CREATE=ON|OFF,ACCESS=ON|OFF,UPDATE=ON|OFF]
SET [HELP]

File attributes work on CP/M and DOS media through BDOS. F1-F4 are CP/M
file flags, not four additional FAT bits. CP/M ARCHIVE=ON means already
archived and clears DOS's archive-needed bit; OFF sets that DOS bit.

Labels, XFCB passwords and timestamp settings require CP/M media. Enable
drive protection before assigning passwords or a default password:
  SET A: [PROTECT=ON]
  SET A:*.TXT [PASSWORD=SECRET,PROTECT=READ]

READ protects reading, writing, renaming and deletion; WRITE protects
writing, renaming and deletion; DELETE protects renaming and deletion;
NONE removes file protection. The default password is inherited by
later commands. Password input supports Backspace, Ctrl-X and Ctrl-C.

To clear passwords, use an empty value followed by another option:
  SET A:FILE.TXT [PASSWORD=,PROTECT=NONE]
  SET A: [PASSWORD=,PROTECT=OFF]
[PASSWORD=] alone is rejected by the historical parser.

CREATE and ACCESS timestamps are mutually exclusive. Enabling either
replaces the other; selecting both together is invalid. INITDIR must
format the directory before timestamping can be enabled. UPDATE is
independent. A bare filespec is not a query: use SDIR or SHOW to inspect.

///1SETPORT
SETPORT
Configures installed serial ports for modems, printers and terminals.
Run without arguments for its menus. Select the port, then configure
baud rate, data bits, stop bits, parity and handshaking to match the
connected device. Only installed ports are offered.

SETPORT changes the running configuration. Use SETUP to save serial-port
settings as boot defaults in CCPM.SYS. This is the retained OEM utility;
its original detailed reference remains in base/help.hlp in the source
tree. Refer to its menus for the complete supported parameter choices.

///1SETUP
SETUP
Menu-driven OEM system configuration. It can save function-key and
serial-port settings, change disk head-step time, install additional
terminals or an MDisk, configure floppy write verification and set a
maximum program-memory allocation.

SETUP modifies CCPM.SYS for use at the next boot. That file must be
available when saving changes. Use the menus for supported settings.
This distribution retains the original 4.1 SETUP binary; it is not the
older CCP/M CONFIG utility. The complete original reference is retained
in base/help.hlp in the source tree.

///1SHOW
    Syntax:

    SHOW {d:}{option}

    SHOW by itself displays the drive, the Read Only  or  Read  Write
    mode  for the optionally specified drive, and the remaining space
    in kilobytes for all logged-in drives in the system.

    Use the SHOW options to  display  drive  characteristics,  active
    user  numbers,  or the directory label. SHOW HELP displays a list
    of available options. SPACE and DRIVE work on CP/M and DOS media.
    USERS and LABEL require CP/M media; DOS disks report that restriction.
    Default SPACE listings omit N:/O:/P:, as in the OEM SHOW command.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    SHOW
    SHOW SPACE
    SHOW DRIVES
    SHOW USERS
    SHOW LABEL
    SHOW HELP
    SHOW A:SPACE
    SHOW B:DRIVE
    SHOW C:USERS
    SHOW D:LABEL

    ///2OPTIONS
    Options:

    SHOW SPACE    ; Same as the SHOW display.

    SHOW DRIVES   ; Displays the drive characteristics of logged-in
                  ; drives on the system, or for a specified drive.

    SHOW USERS    ; Displays the current user number and all user areas
                  ; on the drive that have files assigned to them.

    SHOW LABEL    ; Returns a display of the optional directory label,
                  ; if it has been created.

    SHOW HELP     ; Displays a list of the SHOW options.

    SHOW d:       ; SHOW with the optional drive specifier displays
                  ; information for the specified drive only.

///1SPL
SPL starts the OEM spooler. It uses the SPLIN/SPLOUT and MXSPL queues and
the supplied INDEX.DSP data. PRINTMGR manages spool requests; DSP displays
spool information. These are separate from the direct PRINT command.

///1STOP
  See FREEMEMORY.

///1SUBMIT
    Syntax:

    SUBMIT filespec (optional parameters)

    Explanation:

    SUBMIT  processes  a  command  file  with  a  filetype   of  .SUB
    consisting  of  Concurrent  CP/M  commands  given one command per
    line.   Any  optional  parameters  (such  as a drive or filespec)
    following  the  filespec  in the command line are substituted for
    their corresponding formal parameters ($1,$2,$3...) in the SUBMIT
    file.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>SUBMIT START
    A>SUBMIT B:START
    A>SUBMIT MAX.SUB data1 data2 data3

///1SUSPEND
  Syntax:
  SUSPEND=ON|OFF

  Explanation:
  SUSPEND  causes  Concurrent to suspend the  operation  of  any  DOS programs
  while   those  programs  occupy  a  background  window.   SUSPEND works on a
  per-window  basis.   SUSPEND  has two settings,  ON and  OFF.   The  default
  setting is ON.

  Many  DOS  programs  do not follow DOS  conventions  for  sending characters
  to   the  video display.    When these  programs   output  characters  while
  occupying  a  background  window,  they can  write  through  the  foreground
  window to display characters  on your screen.    This  "writing through"  is
  not a threat  to  your data.  It can, however, be distracting.

  Most   of   the   applications  you  will   use   with   Concurrent  respond
  immediately  to your commands.    For example,   a word  processing  program
  displays    characters   immediately  after   you   press appropriate  keys.
  Because  of  the  interactive  nature of most of the  popular  applications,
  having SUSPEND set to ON is really more of  a  precaution  than a necessity.
  If you  discover  that  an application does not output characters when it is
  not switched in to the foreground, you can set SUSPEND to OFF.

///1SYS
SYS {text}
This compatibility built-in is accepted and ignored. It does not copy
system files or select the system search path. Use SYSDISK for that path.

///1SYSDISK
  Use the SYSDISK command to show the current system disk,  to set a different
  system disk, or to turn off the system disk search sequence.

  Syntax:
  SYSDISK
  SYSDISK d:
  SYSDISK {d:}subname{\subname\subname ...}
  SYSDISK [ON]|[OFF]

  Explanation:
  The first form of the command asks SYSDISK which drive is the current system
  disk.  The other forms make a different drive or subdirectory the new system
  disk  and  turn Concurrent's system disk file searching feature OFF and  ON.
  System disk assignments made with SYSDISK apply to the current window  only.
  If  you  need  to change the system disk on a  regular  basis,  include  the
  SYSDISK command in your STARTUP.BAT files for each window.

  You  can  designate any physical DOS or CP/M disk as the  system  disk.   In
  addition,  floating drives N and O, memory drive M, and any subdirectory can
  be  made the system disk.   When a CP/M disk is designated the system  disk,
  Concurrent  searches in the current user number and user number 0  until  it
  finds  the designated file.   When a DOS disk is designated the system disk,
  Concurrent searches only in the root directory or designated subdirectory.

  Concurrent  assumes that the root directory is designated as the system disk
  until  you  run SYSDISK to specify otherwise.   To make a  subdirectory  the
  system disk,  you must specify all subdirectories in its path.  For example,
  to  make  subdirectory  SUB3  on  drive C  (with  the  path  from  the  root
  directory--SUB1\SUB2\SUB3--the system disk, enter the following:

  C>SYSDISK \SUB1\SUB2\SUB3

  The  backslash  before  SUB1 indicates that the path starts  from  the  root
  directory rather than the current directory.

  To  make the root directory the system disk from a subdirectory,  enter  the
  following:

  C>SYSDISK

  File  searching is automatic when Concurrent is started.   You can turn  off
  this feature on a window-by-window basis by entering the following:

  A>SYSDISK [OFF]

  When  the file searching option is off,  Concurrent searches no further than
  the current directory for all files specified.   To turn on  file  searching
  after  it  has  been  turned off on  a  window-by-window  basis,  enter  the
  following:

  A>SYSDISK [ON]

  Making an MDISK the system disk can provide performance benefits, especially
  for  floppy-disk  systems.   Because you must copy files to the MDISK  every
  time Concurrent is started,  build a BATCH file that transfers the  programs
  to  Drive M and then runs SYSDISK to change the system disk.   The following
  example illustrates the command sequence:

  A>PIP M:=A:*.CMD[R]
  A>SYSDISK M:<Enter>

  The  PIP [R] option is necessary in this example because otherwise PIP  does
  not  copy files with the SYS attribute.

///1systat
    Syntax:

    SYSTAT
    SYSTAT {[option]}
    SYSTAT {[option {C} {n}]}

    Purpose:

    The  SYSTAT  utility shows the internal state of Concurrent CP/M-
    86.  It is useful for  program  and  system  development.  SYSTAT
    displays  memory  allocation,  current  processes,  system  queue
    activity,  and  many  parameters  associated  with  system   data
    structures. SYSTAT  can  present  either  a  static  picture or a
    continuous, real-time display of these system parameters.

    ///2examples
    Examples:

          A>SYSTAT

    This command invokes  the  menu-driven  feature  of  SYSTAT.  The
    utility responds by displaying the following menu:

          Which Option ?

             H(elp)
             M(emory)
             O(verview)
             P(rocesses - All)
             Q(ueues)
             U(ser Processes)
             C(onsoles)
             E(xit)

          ->_

    Typing the appropriate letter in response to the menu obtains the
    associated  display. To  use SYSTAT without the menu, specify the
    menu option letter in the command, like this:

          A>SYSTAT [O] 	       ; this command displays a snapshot
                               ; overview of the system.
          A>SYSTAT [M C]       ; this command displays a continuous
                               ; picture of memory allocation.
          A>SYSTAT [U C 5]     ; this command displays snapshots
                               ; of the user processes every 5 seconds.

    ///2options
    [H]     Displays help for SYSTAT command.

    [M]     Displays all memory potentially available to  users,  but
            does  not  display restricted memory.  The partitions are
            listed in memory-address order.

    [O]     Displays an overview of the system generation parameters.

    [P]     Displays all system processes and resources they use.

    [Q]     Displays  all  queues  and  their  readers,  writers  and
            owners.

    [U]     Displays only user-initiated processes (similar to  [P]).

    [C]     Displays physical console number, virtual console number,
            the name of the process attached to the console, whether
            it is a  foreground  background console, and whether the
            console is in dynamic or buffered mode.

    [E]     Returns you to the system prompt level from the menu.

    [option C n]
            Following  an  option  with a space  and a  C activates a
            continuous display, until you press a key.  Following the
            C with a space and a one  or two-digit  number causes the
            display to update every n seconds.

///1TIME
  Syntax:
  TIME {HH:MM:SS.XX}

  HH is a one- or two-digit number from 0 to 23 for the hour.
  MM is a one- or two-digit number from 0 to 59 for the minute.
  SS is a one- or two-digit number from 0 to 59 for the second.
  XX is a one- or two-digit number from 0 to 99 for the hundredths of a second.

  Explanation:
  TIME lets you display or set the time  on your system.  Concurrent  uses the
  time and date you  enter with  the TIME and DATE commands to keep  track  of
  when you  create, access, or update your files.

  Time is expressed in terms of a 24-hour clock.  If your system has a  status
  line  clock, resetting the time with the TIME command also changes the  time
  displayed on the status line clock.

  On  DOS  media,  space  for  recording times and  dates  of  file  usage  is
  reserved automatically.  For CP/M media, you have the choice of reserving or
  not reserving space for times and dates. To reserve space on CP/M media, use
  the INITDIR and SET utilities.

  ///2EXAMPLES
  To display the current system time,   enter the following command.  You  see
  that  the  system  has  recorded  the  current  time as 2:56:39 p.m.

  A>TIME
  Current time is 14:56:39.23
  Enter new time: _

  If the  preceding time is correct,  press Enter.   If the time is incorrect,
  enter the correct time at the prompt.

  You  do not have to include a leading zero for a.m.  settings.   You also do
  not  have  to include the seconds or hundredths of a  second  fields.   TIME
  considers the following command valid:

  B>TIME
  Current time is 20:19:18.17
  Enter new time: 2:00

  The time is set to 2:00 a.m.

  The following command sets the time to 10:15 p.m.

  A>TIME 22:15

///1TYPE
    Syntax:

    TYPE filespec
    TYPE filespec [PAGE]
    TYPE filespec [PNN]

    Explanation:

    TYPE displays an ASCII text file up to its CP/M Ctrl-Z end marker.
    CP/M files end at Ctrl-Z or a record boundary; DOS files also stop
    at their exact byte length, avoiding final-record padding. A last
    line does not need CR/LF. Wildcards are not accepted.
    [PAGE] pauses after 23 screen rows,
    counting tabs and wrapped text. Paging is off by default.

    The prompt is the native PCTMP PAUSE message:
    Press any key to continue...
    Press a key for the next page, or Ctrl-C to stop. A short file or a
    file ending exactly at the page boundary needs no extra keypress.
    [PNN] selects a smaller page: NN must be two digits, 01 through 23.
    Example: TYPE notes.txt [P10]. [PAGE] uses 23 physical rows.
    Unbracketed P/PNN, [P], [P00] and [P24] are invalid.
    A password can be supplied as filespec;password, or entered when
    prompted. Password entry accepts Backspace, Ctrl-X and Ctrl-C.

    Entering ^P before TYPE echoes output to the printer until the next ^P.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>TYPE letter.dat
    B>TYPE a:document.law [PAGE]
    C>TYPE program.bas [PAGE]
    D>TYPE program.a86

///1USER
    Syntax:

    USER {number 0 - 15}

    Explanation:

    USER displays and changes the current user number.  USER with  no
    command  tail  displays  the  current  user  number.  USER with a
    number from 0 to 15 changes the current user number to the number
    specified  by n.  CP/M assumes a default user number of 0.  Files
    set to SYStem on USER 0 are available to all USER areas as  Read-
    Only.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>USER
    B>USER 0
    A>USER 15
    A>USER 7

///1WINDOW
WINDOW VIEW
WINDOW TOP N=n
WINDOW FULL N=n
WINDOW CHANGE N=n PR=r,PC=c,NR=rows,NC=cols,VR=r,VC=c,TR=ROW,D=COLOR
WINDOW WRITE N=n TYPE=WINDOW|CONSOLE|SETUP,FILE=filespec

CDOS window numbers are 1..4. Screen and virtual coordinates are one-based:
rows 1..24, columns 1..80. CHANGE validates its fields before changing XIOS;
extents are clipped to the screen. VIEW reports all windows; TOP brings a
window forward; FULL expands it to the screen.

CHANGE fields (abbreviations allowed):
  NUMBER/N       PC window number 1..4
  PROW/PR        Physical top row
  PCOL/PC        Physical left column
  NROWS/NR       Window height
  NCOLS/NC       Window width
  VROW/VR        First visible row in the virtual console
  VCOL/VC        First visible column in the virtual console
  TRACKING/TR    NO or ROW cursor tracking
  DISPLAY/D      B&W or COLOR
  FGCOLOR/FG     Foreground color
  BGCOLOR/BG     Background color
Colors: BLACK BLUE GREEN CYAN RED MAGENTA YELLOW WHITE.

WRITE WINDOW saves the visible virtual-screen rectangle. CONSOLE saves
all 24 rows. Both trim trailing spaces. SETUP saves command lines for
all four windows, even though a valid N is still required. Output is
text ending at Ctrl-Z; disk-record padding is not part of the text.

  WINDOW CH N=1 PR=2,PC=4,NR=5,NC=30,FG=WHITE,BG=BLUE
  WINDOW WRITE N=1 TYPE=SETUP,FILE=B:WINDOWS.SUB

WINDOW does not require WMENU to be installed. The resident manager is
a separate interactive interface to the same window services.

///1WMENU
WMENU
Installs and detaches the resident window manager. Once installed,
Ctrl-Alt-Esc opens its menu; Ctrl-keypad-Plus remains available.

Arrows or Space select a highlighted item; Return opens it:
  Place     Move the window with arrows
  Size      Change its dimensions with arrows
  Scroll    Change its view into the virtual console
  Tracking  Toggle NO/ROW with Up/Down or Space
  Display   Toggle B&W/COLOR with Up/Down or Space
  Color     Left/Right selects foreground/background; Up/Down changes it
  Write     Save Window, Console or Setup output
  Abort     Return confirms unloading; Esc returns to the menu

Window numbers are 1..4. Esc or Return leaves an editing submenu with
live changes in effect. Esc from the main menu hides it without unloading.
Write uses a default d:WINDOWn.TXT filename; Return writes, Esc cancels.
Ctrl-Alt-1..4 and Ctrl-Alt-Tab remain the console-switch shortcuts.
WINDOW provides the nonresident command-line interface.
